{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.chirality_frobenius_self_pairing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chirality_frobenius_self_pairing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.chirality_amphichiral_via_qInt3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.chirality_amphichiral_via_qInt3","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/chirality_frobenius_self_pairing.lean#L84-L97","doc":"**Amphichiral chirality factor of the figure-eight** as a quantum\ninteger.\n\nThe manuscript's amphichiral chirality factor is `a_{4_1} = 1 − h²`.\nWriting it through the balanced quantum integer `[3]_q`, the closed\nform is `1 − h² = 4 − [3]_q` (since `[3]_q = q² + 1 + q⁻²` and\n`h² = q² − 2 + q⁻²`). This is the normalisation-free algebraic face\nof the chirality factor; like the Fricke identity it is a multiple of\nthe unit relation, here `+2·(q·q⁻¹ − 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.chirality_figure_eight_character","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.chirality_figure_eight_character","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/chirality_frobenius_self_pairing.lean#L72-L82","doc":"**Figure-eight Fricke identity** (manuscript, amphichiral case).\n\nThe figure-eight character in the standard `(2,1)` representation of\n`H_3(R, q)` is `χ_{4_1}((2,1)) = 2 − [3]_q`, and this equals `−1 − h²`\nwith `h = q − q⁻¹`. The identity is exactly `−2·(q·q⁻¹ − 1)`, i.e. a\nmultiple of the unit relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.qInt3_eq_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.qInt3_eq_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/chirality_frobenius_self_pairing.lean#L59-L70","doc":"**Bridge to the canonical bracket**, restated for the unit-valued `qInt3`.\n\nThe canonical `qIntBal` is a guarded division and so stays over a field; the\nbridge is where this file meets it.  Of the two hypotheses the field-valued\nform carried, `q ≠ 0` is now discharged by the `Units` structure, leaving\n`q − q⁻¹ ≠ 0` — the genuine one, guarding the bracket's denominator — as the\nonly hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.heckeH","kind":"def","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/chirality_frobenius_self_pairing.lean#L55-L57","doc":"The Hecke deformation parameter `h = q − q⁻¹` (AGENTS.md §7a), again with\nthe inverse supplied by the unit structure. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.qInt3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ChiralityFrobenius.qInt3","kind":"def","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/chirality_frobenius_self_pairing.lean#L49-L53","doc":"The balanced quantum integer `[3]_q = q² + 1 + q⁻²`.\n\n`q` is a unit of `R`, so `q⁻²` is the square of the `Units` inverse — a\ndivision-free Laurent expression over `CommRing`, not a field inverse. ","signatureChunk":0}]}